          "bbox": [
            0.603,
            0.044,
            0.848,
            0.061
          ],
          "content": "Describing Motion Around Us"
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.856,
            0.018,
            0.952,
            0.088
          ],
          "content": "N 59"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.088,
            0.402,
            0.109
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "4.2.2 Position-time graphs"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.111,
            0.775,
            0.18
          ],
          "content": "The position-time graph represents the motion of object, i.e., the change in its position with time. You have already plotted two position-time graphs (Figs. 4.11c and 4.12). Now, what information can you obtain from these graphs about the motion of the object?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.182,
            0.773,
            0.216
          ],
          "content": "**What does the shape of the position-time graph indicate about the nature of motion?**"
        },
        {
          "type": "chart",
          "bbox": [
            0.428,
            0.223,
            0.79,
            0.557
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.28,
                0.563,
                0.63,
                0.579
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 4.13: Position-time graph of a moving object"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_11_chart_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.587,
            0.775,
            0.656
          ],
          "content": "As we can see in Fig. 4.13a, a straight line position-time graph indicates that the object is moving with a constant velocity. On the other hand, a curved position-time graph as in Fig. 4.13b, indicates that the velocity is not constant, and thus, the object is in accelerated motion."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.658,
            0.776,
            0.714
          ],
          "content": "**Which physical quantities can be obtained from a position-time graph?** From the position-time graph, you can find the position of an object at each instant of time. Does the graph provide information about any other"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.713,
            0.673,
            0.764
          ],
          "continues_prev": true,
          "content": "physical quantity as well? In fact, you can also calculate how fast the position is changing, i.e., the magnitude of velocity of the object. How can you do that?"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.768,
            0.404,
            0.787
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Activity 4.4: Let us calculate"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.12,
            0.789,
            0.671,
            0.858
          ],
          "content": "1. In the position-time graph we plotted (Fig. 4.11c), consider a part (say, AB) of the graph as shown in Fig. 4.14. From A, draw a line parallel to X-axis and another line parallel to Y-axis. Repeat the same from B."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.121,
            0.861,
            0.67,
            0.931
          ],
          "content": "2. Extend the horizontal line from A and a triangle ABC is formed. What do the sides BC and CA of the triangle represent? BC represents the change in position $( s _ { 2 } -   s _ { 1 } ) ,$ and AC represents the change in time $( t _ { _ 2 } - t _ { _ 1 } )$"
        },
        {
          "type": "chart",
          "bbox": [
